{"id":"references/Petersen/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch01_RiemannianMetrics#def:pet-ch1-riemannian-isometry","title":"def:pet-ch1-riemannian-isometry","path":"references/Petersen/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch01_RiemannianMetrics.md","line":29,"body":"","kind":"definition","status":"not_started","tex":"references/Petersen/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch01_RiemannianMetrics.tex#def:pet-ch1-riemannian-isometry","uses":["[[references/Petersen/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch01_RiemannianMetrics#def:pet-ch1-riemannian-manifold]]"],"inspired_by":[],"bindings":[],"is_index":false,"body_html":"","formal":[],"tex_content":{"path":"references/Petersen/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch01_RiemannianMetrics.tex","label":"def:pet-ch1-riemannian-isometry","line":42,"source":"\\begin{definition}[Riemannian isometry]\n  \\label{def:pet-ch1-riemannian-isometry}\n\n  \\source{petersen:page-0021}\n  \\uses{def:pet-ch1-riemannian-manifold}\n\n  A diffeomorphism $F:(M,g_M)\\to(N,g_N)$ is a \\emph{Riemannian isometry} if\n  $F^*g_N=g_M$, equivalently\n  \\[\n    g_N(DF_pv,DF_pw)=g_M(v,w)\n  \\]\n  for every $p\\in M$ and $v,w\\in T_pM$. Its inverse is again a Riemannian\n  isometry.\n\\end{definition}","html":" <p></p><section class=\"theorem\"><div class=\"theorem-label\">Definition</div></section>[Riemannian isometry] <span class=\"tex-label\" id=\"tex-label-6465663a7065742d6368312d7269656d616e6e69616e2d69736f6d65747279\"></span> <p></p><span class=\"tex-source\" style=\"display:block\">Source: <span class=\"reference\">petersen:page-0021</span></span> <a class=\"wikilink tex-use\" href=\"#node=references%2FPetersen%2Fblueprint%2Fsrc%2Fchapters%2FCh01_RiemannianMetrics%23def%3Apet-ch1-riemannian-manifold\">↗ def:pet-ch1-riemannian-manifold</a> <p></p>A diffeomorphism <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"false\" data-tex=\"$F:(M,g_M)\\to (N,g_N)$\">$F:(M,g_M)\\to (N,g_N)$</span> is a <em>Riemannian isometry</em> if <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"false\" data-tex=\"$F^*g_N=g_M$\">$F^*g_N=g_M$</span>, equivalently <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"true\" data-tex=\"\\[  g_N(DF_pv,DF_pw)=g_M(v,w)  \\]\">\\[  g_N(DF_pv,DF_pw)=g_M(v,w)  \\]</span> for every <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"false\" data-tex=\"$p\\in M$\">$p\\in M$</span> and <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"false\" data-tex=\"$v,w\\in T_pM$\">$v,w\\in T_pM$</span>. Its inverse is again a Riemannian isometry. ","warnings":[]},"previous":"references/Petersen/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch01_RiemannianMetrics#ex:pet-ch1-euclidean-space","next":"references/Petersen/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch01_RiemannianMetrics#ex:pet-ch1-inner-product-space","relations":[{"kind":"uses","id":"references/Petersen/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch01_RiemannianMetrics#def:pet-ch1-riemannian-manifold","title":"def:pet-ch1-riemannian-manifold","label":"def:pet-ch1-riemannian-manifold"}],"backlinks":[{"kind":"uses","id":"references/Petersen/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch01_RiemannianMetrics#ex:pet-ch1-inner-product-space","title":"ex:pet-ch1-inner-product-space","label":"ex:pet-ch1-inner-product-space"},{"kind":"uses","id":"references/Petersen/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch01_RiemannianMetrics#def:pet-ch1-isometry-group","title":"def:pet-ch1-isometry-group","label":"def:pet-ch1-isometry-group"},{"kind":"uses","id":"references/Petersen/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch01_RiemannianMetrics#ex:pet-ch1-isometry-group-euclidean","title":"ex:pet-ch1-isometry-group-euclidean","label":"ex:pet-ch1-isometry-group-euclidean"},{"kind":"uses","id":"references/Petersen/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch01_RiemannianMetrics#def:pet-ch1-left-invariant-metric","title":"def:pet-ch1-left-invariant-metric","label":"def:pet-ch1-left-invariant-metric"}],"copy_context":{"version":1,"level":0,"node":"references/Petersen/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch01_RiemannianMetrics#def:pet-ch1-riemannian-isometry","title":"def:pet-ch1-riemannian-isometry","tex":"\\begin{definition}[Riemannian isometry]\n  \\label{def:pet-ch1-riemannian-isometry}\n\n  \\source{petersen:page-0021}\n  \\uses{def:pet-ch1-riemannian-manifold}\n\n  A diffeomorphism $F:(M,g_M)\\to(N,g_N)$ is a \\emph{Riemannian isometry} if\n  $F^*g_N=g_M$, equivalently\n  \\[\n    g_N(DF_pv,DF_pw)=g_M(v,w)\n  \\]\n  for every $p\\in M$ and $v,w\\in T_pM$. Its inverse is again a Riemannian\n  isometry.\n\\end{definition}","source":"tex/references/Petersen/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch01_RiemannianMetrics.tex","description":"","dependencies":[{"node":"references/Petersen/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch01_RiemannianMetrics#def:pet-ch1-riemannian-manifold","title":"def:pet-ch1-riemannian-manifold","description":""}],"dependencies_omitted":0,"unresolved_dependencies":[],"unresolved_omitted":0}}